comments.text:
@StevenCrowder good video and I vehemently support more open conversations. Unfortunately the students did a poor job at explaining the advantages of studying in the US which accrue not only to the students but also to the American economy. International students benefiting from an education in US universities is only 50% of the transaction. they are paying ~$200k for 2 years of education which is $$ going into the US economy. Not to mention the millions of $$ of value they would create for their employers and the reflexive growth of the American tech industry.

I am a free speech absolutist and I'm very glad you made this video. However, the title is inconsistent with the content of the video. 

Your concerns around the dilution of an American identity are valid and I'm not trying to say I am right/you are wrong. However, when you asked the students "would you consider yourself American," you should also ask yourself (or whoever you are speaking for when you say "some people think only Americans should be able to get a job in America") "would you?"
